// Marko — the Book of Legends: the historical Marko, the songs about him, Šarac, Ravijojla, Musa,
// and the whole song "Marko Kraljević i Musa Kesedžija" with an English translation, line for line.
// Each chapter has its text in both languages; the book shows the one the player chose.
const CHAPTERS=[
 {id:'marko',img:'assets/cast/marko.png',cy:'Краљевић Марко',
  sr:{title:'Kraljević Marko',sub:'istorija · 1335–1395',text:'Marko Mrnjavčević rođen je oko 1335. godine kao sin kralja Vukašina, savladara cara Uroša. Posle Maričke bitke 1371, u kojoj su mu poginuli otac i stric Uglješa, Marko je vladao Prilepom i zapadnom Makedonijom, ali kao vazal turskog sultana. Poginuo je 1395. na Rovinama, boreći se u sultanovoj vojsci protiv vlaškog vojvode Mirče. Konstantin Filozof beleži da je pred bitku rekao kako moli Boga da pomogne hrišćanima, makar on među prvima poginuo. Iz tog malog, tužnog kraljevstva izrastao je najveći junak naših pesama.'},
  en:{title:'Prince Marko',sub:'history · 1335–1395',text:'Marko Mrnjavčević was born around 1335, the son of King Vukašin, co-ruler of Tsar Uroš. After the Battle of the Maritsa in 1371, where his father and his uncle Uglješa fell, Marko ruled Prilep and western Macedonia — but as a vassal of the Turkish sultan. He died in 1395 at Rovine, fighting in the sultan’s army against Mircea of Wallachia. Constantine the Philosopher records that before the battle he said he prayed to God to help the Christians, even if he himself fell among the first. From that small, sad kingdom grew the greatest hero of our songs.'}},
 {id:'pesme',img:'assets/world/cudo-gusle.webp',cy:'Марко у песми',
  sr:{title:'Marko u pesmi',sub:'guslari i narod',text:'Guslari su od vazala napravili zaštitnika. U pesmama Marko živi trista godina, jaše Šarca i nosi teški topuz šestoper. Pravedan je i plah: brani sirotinju, ukida nepravedne namete, ne trpi laž i ne klanja se sultanu ni kad mu služi. Nije bez mane — ume da bude grub i nagao — ali uvek drži zadatu reč. O njemu pevaju Srbi, Bugari i Makedonci, a Vuk Karadžić je zabeležio desetine pesama u kojima je on glavni junak.'},
  en:{title:'Marko in the Songs',sub:'the gusle players and the people',text:'The gusle players turned a vassal into a protector. In the songs Marko lives for three hundred years, rides Šarac and carries a heavy six-flanged mace. He is just and hot-tempered: he defends the poor, abolishes unjust tolls, cannot bear a lie, and does not bow to the sultan even while serving him. He is not without fault — he can be rough and rash — but he always keeps his given word. Serbs, Bulgarians and Macedonians all sing of him, and Vuk Karadžić recorded dozens of songs with Marko as their hero.'}},
 {id:'sarac',img:'assets/cast/sarac.png',cy:'Шарац',
  sr:{title:'Šarac',sub:'Markov verni konj',text:'Šarac je Markov konj, šaren kao da ga je neko poprskao. Priča se da ga je Marko kupio kao bolesno ždrebe koje niko nije hteo i da ga je othranio vinom. Zato Šarac pije vino iz istog kabla s gospodarom, skače tri koplja u visinu i četiri unapred, a u boju grize i rita kao lav. Bio je s Markom sto šezdeset godina. Na Urvini planini Šarac se prvi put spotakao i zaplakao — i tada je Marko znao da mu je došao kraj.'},
  en:{title:'Šarac',sub:'Marko’s faithful horse',text:'Šarac is Marko’s horse, dappled as if someone had sprinkled him. They say Marko bought him as a sickly foal nobody wanted and raised him on wine. That is why Šarac drinks wine from the same bucket as his master, leaps three spears high and four spears ahead, and in battle bites and kicks like a lion. He was with Marko for a hundred and sixty years. On Mount Urvina Šarac stumbled for the first time and wept — and then Marko knew his end had come.'}},
 {id:'vila',img:'assets/cast/fairy.png',cy:'Вила Равијојла',
  sr:{title:'Vila Ravijojla',sub:'Markova posestrima',text:'Vile su gorske devojke: lepe, krilate i strašne kad se naljute. Ravijojla je ustrelila vojvodu Miloša jer je na Miroču lepše pevao od nje. Marko ju je na Šarcu stigao, udario topuzom i naterao da Milošu zaleči rane. Od tada mu je posestrima: kad Marko u nevolji zavapi, ona mu se javi iz oblaka — kao na Kačaniku, kad ga je Musa oborio na zelenu travu. Ali ga i grdi: „Jesam li ti, bolan, govorila da ne činiš u neđelju kavge?”'},
  en:{title:'Ravijojla the Vila',sub:'Marko’s sworn sister',text:'Vilas are maidens of the mountains: beautiful, winged and terrible in their anger. Ravijojla shot the knight Miloš because he sang more beautifully than she did on Mount Miroč. Marko caught her on Šarac, struck her with his mace and made her heal Miloš’s wounds. Since then she has been his sworn sister: when Marko cries out in trouble, she answers him from the clouds — as at Kačanik, when Musa threw him down into the green grass. But she scolds him too: “Did I not tell you, poor man, never to start a quarrel on a Sunday?”'}},
 {id:'musa',img:'assets/cast/musa.png',cy:'Муса Кесеџија',
  sr:{title:'Musa Kesedžija',sub:'gospodar Kačanika',text:'Musa Arbanas devet godina je služio sultana i nije zaradio ni konja ni oružja. Pijan se zakleo da će zatvoriti primorje — i trezan je to učinio: otimao je carevo blago i vešao hodže. Sultan je tada iz tamnice izveo Marka, koji je tamo trunuo tri godine. Na Kačaniku su se borili kopljem, sabljom, topuzom i golim rukama, do podne. Kad je Marko na kraju rasporio Musu, našao je u njemu tri srca, a na trećem je spavala guja. „Jaoh mene”, zaplakao je Marko, „đe pogubih od sebe boljega!”'},
  en:{title:'Musa Kesedžija',sub:'lord of Kačanik',text:'Musa the Albanian served the sultan for nine years and earned neither horse nor weapons. Drunk, he swore he would close the coast — and sober, he did it: he seized the sultan’s treasure and hanged his hodjas. So the sultan brought Marko out of the dungeon, where he had been rotting for three years. At Kačanik they fought with spears, sabres, maces and bare hands until noon. When at last Marko cut Musa open, he found three hearts in him, and on the third a snake lay asleep. “Woe is me,” Marko wept, “for I have killed a better man than I!”'}},
 {id:'uros',img:'assets/world/cudo-carska-knjiga.webp',cy:'Урош и Мрњавчевићи',
  sr:{title:'Uroš i Mrnjavčevići',sub:'pravda pre krvi',text:'Na Kosovu su se posvađala braća Mrnjavčevići — kralj Vukašin, despot Uglješa i vojvoda Gojko — ko će naslediti carstvo posle cara Dušana. Pozvali su protopopa Nedeljka, a on ih je uputio Marku, koji je pisao carske knjige. Marko je dojahao i presudio: carstvo pripada mladom Urošu, „ni po babu, ni po stričevima, već po pravdi Boga istinoga”. Vukašin je potegao zlatan nož na sina, a Marko se sklonio iza crkve. Otac ga je prokleo, a car Uroš blagoslovio: „Lice ti se na sudu svetlilo!”'},
  en:{title:'Uroš and the Mrnjavčević Brothers',sub:'justice before blood',text:'On the field of Kosovo the Mrnjavčević brothers — King Vukašin, Despot Uglješa and Voivode Gojko — quarreled over who should inherit the empire after Tsar Dušan. They called the archpriest Nedeljko, who sent them to Marko, the writer of the imperial letters. Marko rode in and gave his judgment: the empire belongs to young Uroš, “not for the father, nor for the uncles, but by the justice of the true God.” Vukašin drew a golden knife on his son, and Marko took shelter behind the church. His father cursed him, and Tsar Uroš blessed him: “May your face shine at the Judgment!”'}},
 {id:'oranje',img:'assets/world/cudo-zlatno-ralo.webp',cy:'Орање Марка Краљевића',
  sr:{title:'Oranje Marka Kraljevića',sub:'majka Jevrosima',text:'Markova majka Jevrosima mudra je i blaga. Jednom mu je rekla da ostavi čete i boj, da uzme volove i plug, pa da ore brda i doline. Marko je poslušao — ali je zaorao careve drumove. Kad su naišli janičari sa tri tovara blaga i viknuli mu da im ne kvari drum, Marko ih je pobio, a blago doneo majci: „Eto, majko, šta sam danas izorao.”'},
  en:{title:'The Plowing of Prince Marko',sub:'his mother Jevrosima',text:'Marko’s mother Jevrosima is wise and gentle. Once she told him to give up raiding and fighting, to take the oxen and the plow, and to plow the hills and valleys. Marko obeyed — but he plowed the sultan’s roads. When janissaries came by with three loads of treasure and shouted at him not to spoil their road, Marko killed them and brought the treasure to his mother: “There, mother, is what I plowed today.”'}},
 {id:'ramazan',img:'assets/world/cudo-pehar-vina.webp',cy:'Марко пије уз рамазан вино',
  sr:{title:'Vino uz ramazan',sub:'Marko pred sultanom',text:'Sultan je zabranio da se za ramazana pije vino, nosi zelena dolama i igra kolo. Marko je baš tada pio vino, nosio zelenu dolamu i terao hodže i hadžije da igraju s njim. Kad su ga tužili, sultan ga je pozvao, a Marko je seo pred njega s topuzom na kolenima. Mirno je rekao da mu njegova vera dopušta vino, a da je zelena dolama za mlade junake. Sultan je na kraju odustao od kazne i dao mu dukate — da pije vino.'},
  en:{title:'Wine at Ramadan',sub:'Marko before the sultan',text:'The sultan forbade drinking wine, wearing green cloaks and dancing the kolo during Ramadan. That was just when Marko drank wine, wore a green cloak and made the hodjas and hajjis dance with him. When they complained, the sultan summoned him, and Marko sat down before him with his mace across his knees. Calmly he said that his own faith allows wine, and that a green cloak suits a young hero. In the end the sultan gave up on punishing him and gave him ducats — to drink wine.'}},
 {id:'svadbarina',img:'assets/world/cudo-kljuc-kule.webp',cy:'Марко укида свадбарину',
  sr:{title:'Marko ukida svadbarinu',sub:'Arapin i njegova kula',text:'Arapin prekomorac zakupio je od cara Kosovo i udario namet: ko se udaje, trideset dukata; ko se ženi, trideset i četiri. Pa još i gore: na noć mu vode mladu i devojku. Marko je na vodi Servani sreo Kosovku devojku, osedelu od čekanja, dao joj trideset dukata i otišao pred Arapov šator, oko koga je avlija okićena sa sedamdeset i sedam glava. Bacio je pred njega tri dukata. Arapin ga udari topuzom tri-četiri puta, a Marko se nasmeja: „Il’ se šališ, il’ od zbilje biješ?” Jednim udarcem skide mu glavu, a četvoricu slugu ostavi da po Kosovu telale: „Marko za sve svadbarinu plati.” (U igri Arapin drži kulu kraj mora: ta kula je iz druge pesme, „Marko Kraljević i Arapin”, gde crni Arapin iz kule od dvadeset tavana prosi carevu kćer.)'},
  en:{title:'Marko Abolishes the Wedding Tax',sub:'the Arab and his tower',text:'An Arab from over the sea leased Kosovo from the sultan and laid a toll on it: thirty ducats for every bride, thirty-four for every groom. And worse: each night a bride and a maiden were brought to him. By the water of Servana Marko met a girl of Kosovo, grey-haired from waiting, gave her thirty ducats and rode to the Arab’s tent, whose yard was hung with seventy-seven heads. He threw down three ducats. The Arab struck him with his mace three or four times, and Marko laughed: “Are you joking, or striking in earnest?” With one blow he took off the Arab’s head, and left four servants alive to cry through Kosovo: “Marko has paid the wedding tax for all.” (In the game the Arab holds a tower by the sea: that tower comes from another song, “Prince Marko and the Arab”, where a black Arab in a tower twenty floors high demands the sultan’s daughter.)'}},
 {id:'sablja',img:'assets/world/cudo-oceva-sablja.webp',cy:'Марко познаје очину сабљу',
  sr:{title:'Očeva sablja',sub:'osveta za Vukašina',text:'Dok je služio sultana, Marko je u turskoj vojsci video mladog Turčina sa sabljom sa tri zlatna balčaka i tri skupa kamena. Pitao ga je otkud mu, a Turčin se pohvalio da ju je uzeo od ranjenog junaka kraj Marice. Marko je prepoznao sablju kralja Vukašina i na mestu osvetio oca. Kad ga je sultan pozvao na odgovor, Marko je kazao da je sablja bila očeva, a da ju je nosio njegov ubica.'},
  en:{title:'His Father’s Sabre',sub:'vengeance for Vukašin',text:'While he served the sultan, Marko saw a young Turk in the army wearing a sabre with three golden hilts and three precious stones. He asked where it came from, and the Turk boasted that he had taken it from a wounded hero by the Maritsa. Marko recognized the sabre of King Vukašin and avenged his father on the spot. When the sultan called him to answer for it, Marko said the sabre had been his father’s, and the man wearing it was his father’s killer.'}},
 {id:'soko',img:'assets/world/cudo-sokolovo-pero.webp',cy:'Краљевић Марко и соко',
  sr:{title:'Kraljević Marko i soko',sub:'dobro za dobro',text:'Vezir Murat lovio je sa dvanaest delija, a s njima trinaesti Marko. Na zelenom jezeru vezirov soko nije mogao da uhvati utvu zlatokrilu, a Markov jeste. Vezirov soko htede da mu otme plen, a Markov mu prosu sivo perje. Vezir tada uhvati Markovog sokola, udari ga o jelu i prebi mu desno krilo. Marko mu previ krilo, stiže vezira i ne ostavi ni njega ni njegove delije. U drugoj pesmi soko mu vraća dobro: kad je Marko ležao ranjen kraj druma, doneo mu je vode u kljunu i krilima mu pravio hlad, jer ga je Marko na Kosovu, prebijenih krila, sklonio na jelu da ga konji ne pogaze. Dobro se dobrim vraća.'},
  en:{title:'Prince Marko and the Falcon',sub:'kindness repaid',text:'Vizier Murat went hunting with twelve men, and Marko the thirteenth. On a green lake the vizier’s falcon could not take a golden-winged duck, and Marko’s could. The vizier’s falcon tried to rob it, and Marko’s scattered its grey feathers. Then the vizier seized Marko’s falcon, struck it against a fir and broke its right wing. Marko bound the wing, caught up with the vizier and spared neither him nor his men. In another song the falcon repays him: when Marko lay wounded by the road, it brought him water in its beak and shaded him with its wings, because at Kosovo, when its wings were broken, Marko had set it in a fir so the horses would not trample it. Kindness is repaid with kindness.'}},
 {id:'smrt',img:'assets/world/cudo-prelomljeno-koplje.webp',cy:'Смрт Марка Краљевића',
  sr:{title:'Smrt Marka Kraljevića',sub:'Urvina planina',text:'Rano u nedelju Marko je jahao Urvinom planinom kad se Šarac spotakao i zaplakao. Vila mu je rekla da će umreti, a Marko je u bunaru video svoje lice i znao da je istina. Posekao je Šarca da ne služi Turke i sahranio ga bolje nego brata. Sablju je prelomio na četiri komada, koplje na sedam, a topuz bacio u more. Ostavio je pismo i tri kese dukata: jednu onome ko ga sahrani, drugu crkvama, treću slepima i sakatima. Iguman Vaso ga je odneo u Hilandar i tamo sahranio.'},
  en:{title:'The Death of Prince Marko',sub:'Mount Urvina',text:'Early one Sunday Marko was riding over Mount Urvina when Šarac stumbled and wept. The vila told him he would die, and Marko saw his face in a well and knew it was true. He killed Šarac so the horse would never serve the Turks, and buried him better than a brother. He broke his sabre into four pieces and his spear into seven, and threw his mace into the sea. He left a letter and three purses of ducats: one for whoever buried him, one for the churches, one for the blind and the lame. Abbot Vaso carried him to Hilandar and buried him there.'}},
 {id:'spava',img:'assets/world/cudo-orlovo-pero.webp',cy:'Марко још спава',
  sr:{title:'Marko još spava',sub:'narodno predanje',text:'Narod nije verovao da je Marko umro. Pričalo se da spava u pećini, da Šarac kraj njega jede mahovinu, i da mu se sablja zabodena u stenu malo-pomalo izvlači. Kad sablja sama ispadne, Marko će se probuditi i ponovo izaći na drum. Zato guslari i danas pevaju o njemu kao o živom — a ova igra je još jedna takva pesma. Kažu i ovo: ko na mapi putovanja, slovo po slovo, ispiše njegovo ime, naći će put do te pećine.'},
  en:{title:'Marko Still Sleeps',sub:'folk belief',text:'The people never believed that Marko had died. They said he sleeps in a cave, that Šarac grazes on moss beside him, and that his sabre, driven into the rock, is slowly working its way out. When the sabre falls out on its own, Marko will wake and ride out onto the road again. That is why the gusle players still sing of him as if he were alive — and this game is one more such song. They say this too: whoever spells his name, letter by letter, on the journey map will find the way to that cave.'}},
];
